<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The launcher icon on Android 8 and up.

    An adaptive icon is three separate layers rather than one picture, because
    the launcher owns the shape: it masks them to whatever the device's icon
    shape is (circle, squircle, teardrop, and the user can change it), and it
    slides the foreground against the background as the home screen scrolls.
    A single flat PNG cannot do either, which is why `mipmap-*/ic_launcher.png`
    still exists but is only ever seen on API 24 and 25.

    All three layers are generated from the same painter the app itself draws
    (`lib/core/widgets/shoto_brand_mark.dart`, via
    `tool/generate_brand_assets.dart`) — the background is the slab with its
    corners left square for the launcher to cut, and the foreground is the tray
    and cards alone, sized to sit inside the 66dp the platform guarantees will
    survive any mask.

    The monochrome layer is Android 13's themed icons: the launcher discards
    every colour and tints the remaining alpha with the user's wallpaper
    palette. Providing it is what stops Shoto from being the one icon on a
    themed home screen that is still in full colour. Older versions ignore the
    tag.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
